//****************************************************************************
// $Module type: Package
// $Module name: WinAPIGetKeyNameText
// $Author     : Nils Svedmyr, RDC Tools International, <mailto:support@rdctools.com>
// Web-site    : http://www.rdctools.com
// Created     : 2026-07-28
//
// Description : The name Windows gives a virtual key - "F3", "Ctrl", "Page Up".
//
//               BROUGHT HOME from SciControlLib (mWinAPIGetKeyNameText.pkg), and the leading "m" is
//               gone with it: that prefix meant "module" in a 2001 naming scheme this project does not
//               otherwise use, and the file is named after the one function it contains.
//
//               Its caller is MapVKtoString in cRefactorHotKeys.pkg, which uses it as the FALLBACK for
//               keys its own table does not name. So the two are not rivals: the table overrides
//               Windows where Windows reads badly, and this fills in everything else.
//
//               Why the two-step through MapVirtualKey: GetKeyNameText does not take a virtual key. It
//               takes the lParam of a WM_KEYDOWN, where the SCAN code sits in bits 16-23 - hence the
//               MapVirtualKey to get a scan code and the shift by 65536 to put it in place.
//
//               Unicode throughout (MapVirtualKeyW / GetKeyNameTextW), because a non-English keyboard
//               layout names its keys in its own language and the A variants would mangle them.
//
// This code is distributed in the hope that it will be useful, but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY;
// without even the implied warranty of MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.
//****************************************************************************
#IFNDEF GET_MAPVIRTUALKEY
External_Function MapVirtualKey "MapVirtualKeyW" User32.dll UInteger uCode UInteger uMapType Returns Integer
#ENDIF

#IFNDEF GET_GETKEYNAMETEXT
External_Function GetKeyNameText "GetKeyNameTextW" User32.dll UInteger iKey Pointer pBuff Integer iSize Returns Integer
#ENDIF

// The name of a key, from its virtual key code. "" when Windows has no name for it -
// the caller decides what to show instead.
Function WinAPIGetKeyNameText Global Integer iKey Returns String
    Integer iRet
    String  sBuff
    WString wBuff

    Move (Repeat(Character(0), 1024)) to wBuff
    Move (MapVirtualKey(iKey, 0))     to iKey     // virtual key -> scan code
    Move (iKey * 65536)               to iKey     // scan code -> lParam bits 16-23
    Move (GetKeyNameText(iKey, AddressOf(wBuff), 1024)) to iRet
    If (iRet = 0) ;
        Move "" to sBuff
    Else ;
        Move (CString(wBuff)) to sBuff
    Function_Return sBuff
End_Function
